      "text": "To solve the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\), we need to determine the values of \\(x\\) for which the rational expression is non-negative. Let's proceed with the steps:\n\n### Step 1: Analyze the Denominator\nFirst, we analyze the denominator \\(D(x) = x^2 + 2x + 7\\):\n\\[\nD(x) = x^2 + 2x + 7\n\\]\nFind the discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of the quadratic equation \\(D(x) = 0\\):\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac = 2^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 7 = 4 - 28 = -24\n\\]\nSince \\(\\Delta < 0\\), the quadratic \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) has no real roots and is always positive (since the coefficient of \\(x^2\\) is positive).\n\n### Step 2: Vertex of the Parabola\nThe quadratic \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) opens upwards, indicating a minimum value but not a vertex. However, for our purposes, we don't need the vertex because we'll just focus on sign analysis.\n\n### Step 3: Determine Sign of Denominator\nSince \\(D(x) = x^2 + 2x + 7 > 0\\) for all \\(x\\), the sign of the entire rational expression depends solely on the numerator \\(N(x) = x + 6\\):\n\n- \\(N(x) > 0\\) if \\(x > -6\\)\n- \\(N(x) < 0\\) if \\(x < -6\\)\n\n### Step 4: Analyze Numerator\nThe sign of \\(N(x) = x + 6\\) can be determined by checking the sign of \\(x + 6\\) at key points:\n\n- For \\(x = -6\\):\n  \\[\n  N(-6) = -6 + 6 = 0\n  \\]\n  This means \\(N(x)\\) is zero at \\(x = -6\\).\n\n### Step 5: Determine Over/Under Value and Region Where N(x) is > 0\n- When \\(x > -6\\), \\(N(x) > 0\\). All values of \\(x > -6\\) satisfy the numerator condition.\n- When \\(x < -6\\), \\(N(x) < 0\\). No values of \\(x < -6\\) satisfy the numerator condition.\n\nSince the denominator is always positive, the only valid regions for the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\) are when the numerator is non-negative:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{[-6, \\infty)}\n\\]",
