      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the solution for the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\), we need to analyze the signs of the numerator and the denominator separately.\n\n1. **Analyze the Numerator:**\n   The numerator is \\(x + 6\\). This expression does not have any zeros since \\(x + 6 = 0\\) implies \\(x = -6\\). Therefore, the sign of the numerator can be determined by testing points to the left and right of \\(-6\\):\n   - For \\(x < -6\\), \\(x + 6 < 0\\).\n   - For \\(x > -6\\), \\(x + 6 > 0\\).\n\n2. **Analyze the Denominator:**\n   The denominator is \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\). This quadratic expression does not have real roots because its discriminant is negative:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = (2)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 7 = 4 - 28 = -24 < 0.\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the quadratic \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is always positive for all real values of \\(x\\).\n\n3. **Combine the Results:**\n   Since the denominator is always positive and the numerator \\(x + 6\\) can be either positive or negative depending on the value of \\(x\\), we need to consider the intervals determined by the numerator:\n   - For \\(x < -6\\), the numerator \\(x + 6\\) is negative, so the fraction \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) is negative.\n   - For \\(x > -6\\), the numerator \\(x + 6\\) is positive, so the fraction \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) is positive.\n\n   Thus, the fraction \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) is greater than or equal to zero for \\(x \\ge -6\\).\n\nConclusion:\nThe solution to the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\) is all \\(x\\) such that:\n\\[\n\\boxed{[-6, \\infty)}\n\\]",
