      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": " First, let's try and determine the critical interval(s) of \\( x \\). Critical intervals are those value(s) that makes the numerator or denominator equal to zero. Simplifying the equation, we have:\n\\[ x^2 + 2x + 7 = 0. \\]\n\nWe cannot factorise this expression, so we apply the quadratic formula. Our quadratic is now \\( x^2 + 2x + 7 = 0 \\) and our constants are equal to \\( b = 2 \\) and \\( c = 7 \\). Since \\( b^2 - 4ac = 4 - 28 = -24 < 0 \\), our roots will be:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{4 - 28}}{2} = \\frac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{-24}}{2}.\n\\]\nSince the roots do not exist and we are asked to solve between the roots, but because of the quadratic formula that limits \\( x \\) to the roots, we know this means nothing at the end as \\( x^2 + 2x + 7 \\) cannot be negative, because it is always positive.\n\nOur inequality still asks us for positiveness, so the denominator can never be zero (and negative), and we can disregard the numerator. After these steps, the inequality is simply\n\\[ x^2 + 2x + 7 > 0. \\]\n\nSince \\( x^2 + 2x + 7 \\) is always positive, and we did not exclude anything to make the inequality true, \\( x^2 + 2x + 7 > 0 \\) is always true. Therefore, the interval for \\( x \\) is all real numbers (R) or, in interval notation:\n\\[ ( -\\infty, \\infty }. \\]",
